#: ../decisions/0001-add-three-js-rendering-option.md:9
msgid "Add a three.js Rendering Option to Complement vtk.js"
msgstr ""

#: ../decisions/0001-add-three-js-rendering-option.md:11
msgid "Context and Problem Statement"
msgstr ""

#: ../decisions/0001-add-three-js-rendering-option.md:13
msgid "pyvista-js renders in the browser through [vtk.js](https://vtk.org/), which is the JavaScript port of the VTK visualization pipeline. This choice gives pyvista-js a rendering path that is close to desktop VTK, which is what PyVista itself uses. However, vtk.js is a port of a subset of VTK, and not every VTK feature has been implemented there yet. Some VTK classes and rendering capabilities that desktop PyVista users rely on either do not exist in vtk.js or behave differently, so pyvista-js cannot offer them to users today. [three.js](https://threejs.org/) is a mature, widely deployed WebGL library with strong rendering quality, a large ecosystem, and capabilities that overlap with, but do not equal, vtk.js. Should pyvista-js add three.js as an optional rendering backend to complement the features that vtk.js has not yet implemented from VTK?"
msgstr ""

#: ../decisions/0001-add-three-js-rendering-option.md:15
msgid "Decision Drivers"
msgstr ""

#: ../decisions/0001-add-three-js-rendering-option.md:17
msgid "**VTK feature parity**: Users coming from desktop PyVista expect the VTK features they already use; vtk.js gaps directly limit pyvista-js."
msgstr ""

#: ../decisions/0001-add-three-js-rendering-option.md:18
msgid "**Maintenance cost**: A second rendering backend means a second code path in `ts/renderer.ts`, double the testing surface, and tracking a second upstream project's releases."
msgstr ""

#: ../decisions/0001-add-three-js-rendering-option.md:19
msgid "**Bundle size**: The browser client is loaded per session; shipping two rendering libraries increases download size unless backends are lazy-loaded."
msgstr ""

#: ../decisions/0001-add-three-js-rendering-option.md:20
msgid "**Ecosystem risk**: vtk.js is the canonical JavaScript continuation of the VTK ecosystem, which keeps pyvista-js aligned with upstream VTK development."
msgstr ""

#: ../decisions/0001-add-three-js-rendering-option.md:21
msgid "**Mesh structure simplicity**: three.js models surface geometry with a small set of primitive object types such as `Mesh`, `Points`, `Line`, `LineSegments`, and `Sprite`, which matches the current bridge over `VtkPolyData`: a generic `vtkActor` plus `vtkMapper` pair, with `vtkSphereMapper` as the only special case in `ts/renderer.ts`. The advantage is limited to PolyData-style geometry, so the criterion is weighed per vtk.js dataset type in the table below. The current pyvista-js mesh model stays within that style: `PolyData` is rendered directly and `UnstructuredGrid` is converted to polygon data before rendering. The fewer-concepts benefit therefore holds for the data pyvista-js renders today, but the per-dataset-type translation cost must be reweighed when dataset support grows."
msgstr ""

